  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "Compiler::optimize",
    "file": "skia/src/sksl/SkSLCompiler.cpp",
    "description": "The dead-global removal block (around lines 1595-1618) destroys a GlobalVarDeclaration whenever ProgramUsage::isDead reports a write-only global as dead, even though VariableReferences to that global can still exist in function bodies. The Variable object remains owned by the symbol table and its fDeclaration raw pointer is not cleared, so it points into the freed/poisoned GlobalVarDeclaration. On the next optimization iteration, dead-assignment analysis follows a surviving VariableReference to that Variable; ProgramUsage::get calls Variable::initialValue(), which dereferences the dangling fDeclaration and triggers the use-after-poison in SkSLVariable.cpp:15. The crash site is therefore downstream; the lifetime violation originates in Compiler::optimize's premature global-declaration erasure.",
    "conditions": "The harness treats all but the final 256 bytes as SkSL and invokes SkRuntimeEffect compilation. The PoV code declares global struct variable `t`, has dead function `m` read/write `t.z`, function `wna` write `t.z`, and dead caller `a` call `wna`. With optimization/dead-function and dead-global elimination enabled, the first pass removes `m` and `a`; removing their usage leaves `t` write-only via still-present `wna`, while `wna` only becomes unreferenced after `a` is processed and is not erased until a later pass. In that intermediate state, global `t` is classified dead and its declaration is erased although `wna` still contains `t.z=1`. The next pass scans `wna` before removing it, reaches dead-assignment analysis, and dereferences `t`'s stale declaration pointer."
  },
